s for instance  saltwater crocodiles reproduce quickly  brown bears don't the socio-economic  circumstances of cultural circumstances  there are a lot of differences there are  maybe a few lessons that can cautiously  be extracted but I didn't come away with  any panaceas any any big answers that I  that I want to wave in people's faces  one of the things for instance though  that comes out of looking at the  Romanian situation is that if you have  large packs of really big nasty dogs you  can keep bears away from your sheep most  of the time they have a very different  way of of shepherding in Romania than we  have here it's very labor-intensive in  terms of in terms of human labor the  Shepherd's are out there there's lots of  shepherds they sleep with the Sheep out  on the on the high Meadows literally  wrapped up in a sheepskin I they don't  even walk 100 yards up to the cabin they  sleep out amid the flock and they've got  these big flock  big packs of truly ugly scary dogs that  seem to do a great job of running off  wolves and running off bear so that's  part of it the other thing that comes  out of most of these situations to at  least to my eye is that these people  don't have expectations of perfect  invulnerability from the dangers of the  world the people the mall dari in the  gear forests in India would tell me  things like well the lion is part of the  forest and we want to live in the forest  we don't want to live in Bombay we don't  want to live in the village this is this  is life this is the world and  occasionally we have to tangle with a  lion so be it that's we accept the costs  of doing business in this ecosystem and  there's this acceptance of a certain  degree of danger a certain degree of  loss that it seems natural to them that  that tends to take me into a larger  subject that I won't unravel completely  here but it's it's the idea of different  levels of tolerance for risk among  different kinds of people I think we all  have we all have 