ion on her back to  look at us and and lance is saying to me  David David uncap your bear spray and  I'm thinking wait a minute you're the  one who's name is Craig head how come  I'm the only one who has bear spray  but she made her decision to to back off  and withdraw and not and not punish us  for that that little slip know anybody  else yes ma'am and I'm now thinking that  a backyard without any bears is empty  well sir maybe one more question if  there is one traveling and researching  for this book about three years  traveling and researching and a bet well  maybe two and a half four years  altogether traveling researching and  writing for years I thought it was going  to be a quick easy book as a change of  pace after the song of the dodo and and  before I knew it was for years what one  more um did sacrifice play a role in any  of the people that you came across and  in their lives I sacrifice of humans or  sacrifice of animals or animals humans  are animals not really among any of the  cultures that I've visited and got to  know close up there are there there are  some cultures in which sacrifice either  sacrifice of a human or sacrifice of an  animal is part of their relationship I  think it's the I knew in Japan who have  a relationship with the brown bear again  Ursus arctos I believe in Japan they  call it higuma and they venerate the  brown bear they also periodically  perform this ceremony where they capture  a bear cub a young bear cub they raise  it to the age of two or three and then  they kill it after having taken very  good care of it they kill it and they  call that sending the cub home and it's  some ceremonial part of their  relationship with this one with this  creature higuma there are probably a few  others like that but  among the cultures that I visited there  there was no real pattern or cultural  practice of sacrifice no okay one more  and then studied and written about  predators in Yellowstone Park and do you  know what is happening 